**Definition 4.3 (normalized complete-output error).** The ideal output
$\Omega_h^q$ retains the terminal live quantum block or exact first new
failure time/mode and $f$. It has no acquisition abort or erroneous
rejection sectors. For normalized full classical–quantum outputs use

$$
\operatorname{dist}(\omega,\eta)=\tfrac12\|\omega-\eta\|_{1,\rm cq}.
\tag{FH.12}
$$

The norm sums/integrates trace norms over all classical sectors, using a
common dominating measure when needed. Exact time is a measurable variable,
not an uncountable orthonormal basis or finite-bit archive. Let $e_h(q)$
be the full error, including retained joint fields whenever required.
It dominates
$\operatorname{dist}(\Phi_q(\sigma_h),\Omega_h^q)$ after the stated
forgetting maps. Hence every declared output event differs in probability
by at most $e_h(q)$. No probability below is conditioned on successful
acquisition. Conditioning on the already reached original live cut is
the separately named starting experiment.

These are source-family comparisons, with the same actual history label
and its prescribed target on both sides. They are not an identification
of different unknown inputs inside the common-input instrument metric
of [Instrument Stability][IS]. A complete common-map/reference guarantee
would imply these necessary tests, which already use trivial references.

